# Line-ending policy.
#
# Git stores text with LF, but a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true writes
# CRLF back out. Several of those files are bind-mounted straight into the Linux
# containers, where a CRLF shebang makes the kernel look for an interpreter
# named "/bin/bash\r" and the entrypoint dies with "not found". Pinning eol=lf
# keeps those files usable no matter which platform checked them out.

* text=auto

# Executed inside containers or on the server.
*.sh text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts genuinely want CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.cbz binary
*.cbr binary
*.zip binary
*.pdf binary
*.lockb binary
